WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:06.790
&gt;&gt; It is my great pleasure

00:00:06.791 --> 00:00:09.730
to welcome Tess Posner
to Microsoft today.

00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:12.260
She is the CEO of AI4All,

00:00:12.261 --> 00:00:14.380
an organization that
is trying to increase

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:17.080
diversity inclusion
and reliability of

00:00:17.081 --> 00:00:20.160
AI systems in the world and she's

00:00:20.161 --> 00:00:21.220
going to be talking about

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.605
the wonderful work they
are doing in that space.

00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:26.830
Before joining AI4All,
she was Managing

00:00:26.831 --> 00:00:29.930
Director of TechHire at
Opportunity at Work,

00:00:29.931 --> 00:00:32.770
an organization and should
they have launch out of

00:00:32.771 --> 00:00:35.200
White House to increase

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.540
diversity in the tech economy,
so very relevant.

00:00:37.541 --> 00:00:40.510
Before that, she built
and run summer school and

00:00:40.511 --> 00:00:44.060
non-profit supporting
low-income people

00:00:44.061 --> 00:00:46.195
to find work in
the digital economy.

00:00:46.196 --> 00:00:49.500
Tess's work has been featured
in Business Insider,

00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:51.500
TechCrunch and Fast Company and

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:53.360
she's going to be
talking about how we can

00:00:53.361 --> 00:00:55.125
hopefully collaborate
together to make

00:00:55.126 --> 00:00:57.980
AI much more inclusive
in the future.

00:00:57.981 --> 00:00:59.775
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

00:00:59.776 --> 00:01:02.120
Thank you all and I'm really so

00:01:02.121 --> 00:01:04.520
excited to be here
today and about

00:01:04.521 --> 00:01:07.610
both our existing partnership
with Microsoft as well as

00:01:07.611 --> 00:01:09.305
new opportunities to really

00:01:09.306 --> 00:01:11.160
grow and develop how
we work together.

00:01:11.161 --> 00:01:17.610
To get started, none of
this is a secret to you,

00:01:17.611 --> 00:01:20.880
but obviously artificial
intelligence is really one of

00:01:20.881 --> 00:01:24.965
the most important technologies
of today and tomorrow.

00:01:24.966 --> 00:01:28.005
It's been called
the new electricity,

00:01:28.006 --> 00:01:30.740
the driver of the Fourth
Industrial Revolution,

00:01:30.741 --> 00:01:32.715
and is clearly poised to make

00:01:32.716 --> 00:01:35.825
incredible economic
impact on society,

00:01:35.826 --> 00:01:38.565
as well as get embedded into

00:01:38.566 --> 00:01:40.380
our daily lives and many

00:01:40.381 --> 00:01:42.200
of the things that
Microsoft is working on.

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:47.140
In terms of the incredible impact

00:01:47.141 --> 00:01:48.655
there's also potential for

00:01:48.656 --> 00:01:51.310
AI to create breakthroughs in

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:53.560
moonshots and solve some

00:01:53.561 --> 00:01:55.705
of the world's most
pressing problems.

00:01:55.706 --> 00:02:00.040
This actually came
out of the future

00:02:00.041 --> 00:02:02.110
computed which is
a report coming out

00:02:02.111 --> 00:02:04.425
by Microsoft earlier this year,

00:02:04.426 --> 00:02:06.955
talking about some of
the ways that AI can

00:02:06.956 --> 00:02:10.885
impact the water supply issues,

00:02:10.886 --> 00:02:14.710
agriculture, climate change,
biodiversity and really

00:02:14.711 --> 00:02:17.260
create potential moonshots to

00:02:17.261 --> 00:02:19.060
solve some of these problems.

00:02:19.061 --> 00:02:20.710
There's tremendous potential with

00:02:20.711 --> 00:02:22.630
this exciting technology that

00:02:22.631 --> 00:02:24.460
has been around for awhile but

00:02:24.461 --> 00:02:27.730
because of new developments
were obviously in sort of

00:02:27.731 --> 00:02:30.700
an AI exciting moment

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:32.170
right now and it's getting talked

00:02:32.171 --> 00:02:34.415
about a lot in the media as well.

00:02:34.416 --> 00:02:37.255
However, given that this

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:39.040
is such an important
technology there

00:02:39.041 --> 00:02:40.720
are some key risks to

00:02:40.721 --> 00:02:43.060
both responsible and
ethical development of AI,

00:02:43.061 --> 00:02:45.520
that I know Microsoft
is really focused

00:02:45.521 --> 00:02:48.250
on and has been leading
in these areas.

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:49.705
This includes bias and

00:02:49.706 --> 00:02:51.865
algorithms and
machine learning systems,

00:02:51.866 --> 00:02:54.675
the diversity and
talent crisis in AI,

00:02:54.676 --> 00:02:56.520
both in terms of industry but

00:02:56.521 --> 00:02:58.710
also in research and academia,

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:01.200
education, and really the

00:03:01.201 --> 00:03:03.845
lack of access and
knowledge about AI.

00:03:03.846 --> 00:03:07.135
I want to talk a little bit
more about these issues.

00:03:07.136 --> 00:03:09.790
The first is around
the diversity crisis.

00:03:09.791 --> 00:03:11.700
So, we have heard a lot about

00:03:11.701 --> 00:03:14.590
the diversity crisis in
the tech sector more broadly.

00:03:14.591 --> 00:03:18.135
In AI as a sub-field
of computer science

00:03:18.136 --> 00:03:22.134
it's also pretty extreme and
we call it an actual crisis.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.910
So, some of the data
shows 13 percent of

00:03:24.911 --> 00:03:27.790
AI and machine learning
companies are run by women,

00:03:27.791 --> 00:03:29.950
and the numbers are even more

00:03:29.951 --> 00:03:33.055
problematic and scary frankly.

00:03:33.056 --> 00:03:34.735
If you look at research,

00:03:34.736 --> 00:03:37.170
tenure-track engineering faculty

00:03:37.171 --> 00:03:41.090
less than really
four percent are minorities.

00:03:41.091 --> 00:03:43.720
We also see and we hear a lot of

00:03:43.721 --> 00:03:45.790
this working in
different communities talking

00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:47.905
and trying to expose people to

00:03:47.906 --> 00:03:50.259
AI and just hearing what
are their perceptions,

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:52.120
what do they actually
know about it.

00:03:52.121 --> 00:03:53.770
Some of the perceptions
that are out

00:03:53.771 --> 00:03:56.450
there are that it's
the terminator,

00:03:56.451 --> 00:03:57.660
it's coming to get you,

00:03:57.661 --> 00:03:59.100
it's the robot apocalypse,

00:03:59.101 --> 00:04:02.010
it's scary, it's dangerous
because it's going

00:04:02.011 --> 00:04:03.585
to take over and

00:04:03.586 --> 00:04:06.825
automate all of our jobs
and what's going to happen.

00:04:06.826 --> 00:04:09.480
There's wildly different
predictions about how

00:04:09.481 --> 00:04:12.460
it's going to affect
the economy and the workforce,

00:04:12.461 --> 00:04:15.375
and lastly that it's
exclusive and that you need

00:04:15.376 --> 00:04:19.620
a PhD to get involved and
really that it's not for me.

00:04:19.621 --> 00:04:21.120
This is what we hear from a lot

00:04:21.121 --> 00:04:23.085
of our students at AI4All.

00:04:23.086 --> 00:04:24.595
This is not for me.

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:27.690
So, this perception really
is a problem because it

00:04:27.691 --> 00:04:28.770
prevents people from getting

00:04:28.771 --> 00:04:30.790
involved in this exciting field,

00:04:30.791 --> 00:04:32.505
as well as shaping

00:04:32.506 --> 00:04:33.900
our imaginations about what

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:36.090
is possible with the technology.

00:04:36.091 --> 00:04:38.740
We also see bias creeping up in

00:04:38.741 --> 00:04:40.390
more and more decisions that

00:04:40.391 --> 00:04:42.155
are affecting people's lives.

00:04:42.156 --> 00:04:43.750
Some of you may be familiar with

00:04:43.751 --> 00:04:46.780
this research that came
out several years ago

00:04:46.781 --> 00:04:48.790
about how the software called

00:04:48.791 --> 00:04:51.945
Compas is being used in
the criminal justice system,

00:04:51.946 --> 00:04:56.355
to help judges make sentencing
and parole decisions.

00:04:56.356 --> 00:04:58.325
This software was studied

00:04:58.326 --> 00:05:01.630
10,000 cases of it
used in Florida were

00:05:01.631 --> 00:05:04.630
actually looked at and
there was bias that showed

00:05:04.631 --> 00:05:08.175
up in these risk
score algorithms,

00:05:08.176 --> 00:05:11.410
that were shown to
be more favorable

00:05:11.411 --> 00:05:14.930
towards white
individuals and would

00:05:14.931 --> 00:05:19.610
have higher risk scores

00:05:19.611 --> 00:05:21.170
for African-Americans even with

00:05:21.171 --> 00:05:22.850
lessor criminal
background history.

00:05:22.851 --> 00:05:24.950
So obviously, this is incredibly

00:05:24.951 --> 00:05:27.140
problematic for how
these systems are getting

00:05:27.141 --> 00:05:29.450
embedded and already being
used out in the wild and

00:05:29.451 --> 00:05:32.495
affecting people's lives
in such extreme ways.

00:05:32.496 --> 00:05:35.320
Other bias examples,

00:05:35.321 --> 00:05:37.370
and I'm sure you all are
familiar with many more,

00:05:37.371 --> 00:05:39.980
but this is one that obviously
came out of [inaudible]

00:05:39.981 --> 00:05:42.260
Bruce research from
Microsoft looking

00:05:42.261 --> 00:05:44.740
at facial recognition systems,

00:05:44.741 --> 00:05:46.900
and showing wildly
different levels of

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:48.550
accuracy when you look at

00:05:48.551 --> 00:05:51.085
subgroups including
gender and race.

00:05:51.086 --> 00:05:53.320
Obviously, these
systems are getting

00:05:53.321 --> 00:05:55.850
embedded into hiring processes,

00:05:55.851 --> 00:06:00.560
into the criminal justice
system, into the TSA.

00:06:00.561 --> 00:06:04.510
So, looking at accuracy
and bias is critical as

00:06:04.511 --> 00:06:06.310
these systems are
getting more and

00:06:06.311 --> 00:06:10.135
more prevalent in
our daily lives.

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:14.240
So, this isn't just a problem for

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:21.820
those individuals that are

00:06:21.821 --> 00:06:24.640
actually affected these subgroups

00:06:24.641 --> 00:06:25.820
that we're talking about.

00:06:25.821 --> 00:06:29.040
It's also a problem because
these systems are being used.

00:06:29.041 --> 00:06:31.270
So, a recent Gallup
poll showed that

00:06:31.271 --> 00:06:34.450
85 percent of Americans
use AI everyday.

00:06:34.451 --> 00:06:38.400
This is pretty impressive
and it's just going to grow.

00:06:38.401 --> 00:06:42.960
So, how do we actually
addressed this?

00:06:42.961 --> 00:06:44.620
There are a lot of things

00:06:44.621 --> 00:06:46.210
that I'm really excited to learn

00:06:46.211 --> 00:06:49.370
more about from all of you
that Microsoft is leading on,

00:06:49.371 --> 00:06:52.555
including better systems
of how we test

00:06:52.556 --> 00:06:56.650
monitor and audit AI
systems before and in

00:06:56.651 --> 00:06:59.110
the wild so we can track and

00:06:59.111 --> 00:07:04.225
take actions actually
mitigate some of these.

00:07:04.226 --> 00:07:07.715
Fairness in ethics standards,

00:07:07.716 --> 00:07:10.020
transparency and explainability,

00:07:10.021 --> 00:07:11.110
and I know some of
you in the room are

00:07:11.111 --> 00:07:12.840
actually working on this,

00:07:12.841 --> 00:07:16.620
and of course lastly is
diversity inclusion.

00:07:16.621 --> 00:07:21.100
All of these we consider AI4All
to be really critical and

00:07:21.101 --> 00:07:25.870
important to addressing this
and mitigating these risks,

00:07:25.871 --> 00:07:27.790
but we believe that one of

00:07:27.791 --> 00:07:29.500
the most important things
is to actually

00:07:29.501 --> 00:07:32.040
address diversity
inclusion and AI.

00:07:32.041 --> 00:07:34.810
So that's why we started AI4All,

00:07:34.811 --> 00:07:37.750
which is a non-profit
organization founded to increase

00:07:37.751 --> 00:07:40.890
diversity inclusion
in AI development,

00:07:40.891 --> 00:07:42.495
policy, and research.

00:07:42.496 --> 00:07:45.220
So, not just looking at
AI development but also

00:07:45.221 --> 00:07:48.965
all the different parts that
this technology will touch,

00:07:48.966 --> 00:07:51.820
including the education system,

00:07:51.821 --> 00:07:54.310
government systems and
everyone needing sort

00:07:54.311 --> 00:07:56.770
of not just AI literacy but

00:07:56.771 --> 00:07:58.930
to have more diversity in all of

00:07:58.931 --> 00:08:00.340
these areas that are
going to be shaping

00:08:00.341 --> 00:08:02.375
such important decisions.

00:08:02.376 --> 00:08:05.790
So, I want to share
a little bit about our work.

00:08:05.791 --> 00:08:10.315
So, why did we start AI4ALL?

00:08:10.316 --> 00:08:12.920
There's a challenge
when you're looking at

00:08:12.921 --> 00:08:16.219
barriers to under-represented
populations actually

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.215
getting into the AI field and

00:08:18.216 --> 00:08:20.790
touching the different ways that

00:08:20.791 --> 00:08:23.220
this technology will impact

00:08:23.221 --> 00:08:24.680
all these different areas and

00:08:24.681 --> 00:08:26.900
in an interdisciplinary
perspective.

00:08:26.901 --> 00:08:28.650
So, in terms of the current

00:08:28.651 --> 00:08:30.450
homogenous culture and AI as well

00:08:30.451 --> 00:08:32.580
as some of the perception issues

00:08:32.581 --> 00:08:34.060
that we talked about earlier,

00:08:34.061 --> 00:08:36.459
that doesn't necessarily attract

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.645
new people getting into
the field and can be a barrier.

00:08:39.646 --> 00:08:42.220
There's also a lack of exposure

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:44.735
to technical concepts
and certainly AI.

00:08:44.736 --> 00:08:47.700
So, AI is currently not taught in

00:08:47.701 --> 00:08:49.200
high schools and it's not part

00:08:49.201 --> 00:08:51.360
of the computer
science education.

00:08:51.361 --> 00:08:53.400
In fact, only 40 percent of

00:08:53.401 --> 00:08:56.530
high schools have computer
science education even been

00:08:56.531 --> 00:08:58.210
offered not to mention
that they're not

00:08:58.211 --> 00:09:02.520
including new and emerging
technologies like AI.

00:09:02.521 --> 00:09:05.580
There's a few relatable
role models.

00:09:05.581 --> 00:09:09.305
So, a lot of the reason why
we choose different careers

00:09:09.306 --> 00:09:13.220
and why we want to even
go into these areas,

00:09:13.221 --> 00:09:16.370
is because of who we'd
meet and get exposure to,

00:09:16.371 --> 00:09:19.370
what they do, why they
do it, how they do it.

00:09:19.371 --> 00:09:24.180
If we don't see individuals
that we can relate to,

00:09:24.181 --> 00:09:26.230
it's much harder to picture

00:09:26.231 --> 00:09:28.750
yourself going into that field.

00:09:28.751 --> 00:09:31.330
A lot of these are
actually based on

00:09:31.331 --> 00:09:34.290
a Microsoft research study

00:09:34.291 --> 00:09:35.730
that came out about

00:09:35.731 --> 00:09:37.990
earlier barriers to getting

00:09:37.991 --> 00:09:39.805
into the computer science field.

00:09:39.806 --> 00:09:44.055
So, we actually built
AI4ALL to address each of

00:09:44.056 --> 00:09:46.640
these barriers and
ensure that people

00:09:46.641 --> 00:09:50.255
from all backgrounds are
able to get into the field.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:52.245
There's also this idea of

00:09:52.246 --> 00:09:54.895
peer community and
support that is lacking.

00:09:54.896 --> 00:09:57.570
So, if that is

00:09:57.571 --> 00:10:01.035
not prevailing at your
high school for example,

00:10:01.036 --> 00:10:04.600
it can be much more challenging
to feel like you can go

00:10:04.601 --> 00:10:09.185
into a computer science
club for example,

00:10:09.186 --> 00:10:11.860
that's 100 percent male
if you're a young woman,

00:10:11.861 --> 00:10:13.050
and that's what we
hear from a lot

00:10:13.051 --> 00:10:14.690
of our students as well.

00:10:14.691 --> 00:10:17.200
So, we addressed this with

00:10:17.201 --> 00:10:19.945
three core initiatives
that are really

00:10:19.946 --> 00:10:22.580
trying to not only

00:10:22.581 --> 00:10:25.485
bring more people into
the field to begin with,

00:10:25.486 --> 00:10:28.810
but also to support their
pathway and long-term success.

00:10:28.811 --> 00:10:31.375
So, our first initiative is

00:10:31.376 --> 00:10:33.690
a summer camp program that

00:10:33.691 --> 00:10:37.180
exposes diverse
high school students to

00:10:37.181 --> 00:10:40.000
artificial intelligence and
we partner with some of

00:10:40.001 --> 00:10:42.605
the top research organizations

00:10:42.606 --> 00:10:46.570
including Stanford University,
Princeton, UC Berkeley.

00:10:46.571 --> 00:10:48.760
We actually bring students into

00:10:48.761 --> 00:10:50.090
these research labs over

00:10:50.091 --> 00:10:52.130
the summer for
a two to three week camp,

00:10:52.131 --> 00:10:55.390
and they learn directly
from AI faculty,

00:10:55.391 --> 00:10:59.135
researchers, get
technical exposure to AI,

00:10:59.136 --> 00:11:01.705
get connected with
role models and mentors,

00:11:01.706 --> 00:11:04.635
and then also get to
work on AI projects.

00:11:04.636 --> 00:11:06.410
The second piece is

00:11:06.411 --> 00:11:08.820
increasing awareness and
access to AI and this is

00:11:08.821 --> 00:11:09.965
really getting back to

00:11:09.966 --> 00:11:12.900
the perception issues that
we talked about earlier,

00:11:12.901 --> 00:11:14.990
that AI literacy is really

00:11:14.991 --> 00:11:17.295
becoming something
that needs to be

00:11:17.296 --> 00:11:19.445
basic for almost everyone

00:11:19.446 --> 00:11:22.180
participating in
our society in the future.

00:11:22.181 --> 00:11:23.760
Then lastly research.

00:11:23.761 --> 00:11:25.120
I want to talk a little bit more

00:11:25.121 --> 00:11:27.095
about each of these pieces.

00:11:27.096 --> 00:11:30.060
The first is our
summer camp programs,

00:11:30.061 --> 00:11:32.760
which I talked
a little bit about before.

00:11:32.761 --> 00:11:36.140
So, we last year we had

00:11:36.141 --> 00:11:39.375
summer camps operating at
Stanford and UC Berkeley.

00:11:39.376 --> 00:11:40.865
This summer, we're tripling

00:11:40.866 --> 00:11:42.245
the number of schools
that were at,

00:11:42.246 --> 00:11:45.990
and as you can see each of
these schools focuses on

00:11:45.991 --> 00:11:48.770
a slightly different
population as

00:11:48.771 --> 00:11:52.545
well as different
geographical focus.

00:11:52.546 --> 00:11:55.290
I wanted to share with
each of you a little bit

00:11:55.291 --> 00:11:57.455
more about the
curriculum that we've

00:11:57.456 --> 00:11:59.010
developed in partnership with

00:11:59.011 --> 00:12:02.015
these universities
as well as our team.

00:12:02.016 --> 00:12:05.375
So the first piece, is
technical exposure.

00:12:05.376 --> 00:12:06.970
Our camps are pretty rigorous,

00:12:06.971 --> 00:12:08.360
because we want to give students

00:12:08.361 --> 00:12:10.050
that hands-on exposure to some of

00:12:10.051 --> 00:12:13.635
the key concepts and
importantly ethics

00:12:13.636 --> 00:12:15.920
and the societal
implications of AI are

00:12:15.921 --> 00:12:18.695
really a lens that is throughout

00:12:18.696 --> 00:12:20.705
all of the pieces that we

00:12:20.706 --> 00:12:23.880
share with students even
the technical concepts,

00:12:23.881 --> 00:12:25.115
we're always talking about.

00:12:25.116 --> 00:12:28.370
What kind of bias could
potentially creep up here,

00:12:28.371 --> 00:12:30.690
what are some of
the mitigation strategies,

00:12:30.691 --> 00:12:32.765
and what are the
implications of this will

00:12:32.766 --> 00:12:35.075
have both positive and
negative in the world,

00:12:35.076 --> 00:12:37.070
and what are some
of the frameworks

00:12:37.071 --> 00:12:39.895
that are getting developed.

00:12:39.896 --> 00:12:41.100
Although there's not like a

00:12:41.101 --> 00:12:42.950
consistent framework
out there now,

00:12:42.951 --> 00:12:45.345
we are exposing our students to

00:12:45.346 --> 00:12:48.175
some of these frameworks
getting developed.

00:12:48.176 --> 00:12:51.540
We're also giving them the
opportunity to get involved.

00:12:51.541 --> 00:12:54.280
So on Monday, we're
going to be launching

00:12:54.281 --> 00:12:58.480
the first ever high school
committee with the I Tripoli,

00:12:58.481 --> 00:13:01.855
AI and ethics of
autonomous systems initiative.

00:13:01.856 --> 00:13:03.720
So, young people are
actually going to get

00:13:03.721 --> 00:13:06.160
a chance to be involved in

00:13:06.161 --> 00:13:08.040
this conversation and
shaping what some of

00:13:08.041 --> 00:13:09.400
these standards are and getting

00:13:09.401 --> 00:13:10.950
their perspectives in the mix.

00:13:10.951 --> 00:13:14.160
Since this generation is really
going to be the one that

00:13:14.161 --> 00:13:17.685
is most impacted by the
decisions that we make today.

00:13:17.686 --> 00:13:19.190
It's critical that we involved

00:13:19.191 --> 00:13:21.094
them at these early stages.

00:13:21.095 --> 00:13:24.320
So, the second is soft skills
and career exploration.

00:13:24.321 --> 00:13:26.090
So, again, this is going back to

00:13:26.091 --> 00:13:28.510
the need for role models
and mentors.

00:13:28.511 --> 00:13:30.355
So, we bring in individuals

00:13:30.356 --> 00:13:32.575
from our industry partners and

00:13:32.576 --> 00:13:35.540
all different parts
of AI to really

00:13:35.541 --> 00:13:39.055
understand what kinds of
careers are out there,

00:13:39.056 --> 00:13:41.325
what does it actually
look like day to day,

00:13:41.326 --> 00:13:45.140
and also developing that peer
and mentorship community

00:13:45.141 --> 00:13:47.135
that's so important for students.

00:13:47.136 --> 00:13:49.170
The last piece which
is really exciting

00:13:49.171 --> 00:13:51.040
and I think we've
found has been one of

00:13:51.041 --> 00:13:52.565
the most successful ways that

00:13:52.566 --> 00:13:56.075
engaging diverse groups
of young people in AI,

00:13:56.076 --> 00:13:59.755
is to actually create
AI for good projects.

00:13:59.756 --> 00:14:01.470
So, not only are students getting

00:14:01.471 --> 00:14:04.040
hands-on exposure to practice

00:14:04.041 --> 00:14:06.940
some of the skills that
they've developed earlier on,

00:14:06.941 --> 00:14:10.400
but they also are seeing what are

00:14:10.401 --> 00:14:12.790
the exciting applications of

00:14:12.791 --> 00:14:14.910
this technology for
good and to solve

00:14:14.911 --> 00:14:17.455
some of the big challenges
in the world today.

00:14:17.456 --> 00:14:19.720
We've seen that this has not

00:14:19.721 --> 00:14:21.760
only captured
our students imaginations,

00:14:21.761 --> 00:14:23.280
but that they've gone on to work

00:14:23.281 --> 00:14:25.085
on AI projects afterwards,

00:14:25.086 --> 00:14:28.920
start AI clubs at their schools
and even win awards.

00:14:28.921 --> 00:14:30.780
&gt;&gt; So, just [inaudible].

00:14:30.781 --> 00:14:31.575
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:14:31.576 --> 00:14:33.210
&gt;&gt; For this summer camps that is

00:14:33.211 --> 00:14:35.665
happening in these universities,

00:14:35.666 --> 00:14:38.180
how does production look like?

00:14:38.181 --> 00:14:40.285
Do some of them focus

00:14:40.286 --> 00:14:42.575
on research and some
of them are more on

00:14:42.576 --> 00:14:46.000
the curriculum for learning
about these topics or it's

00:14:46.001 --> 00:14:50.365
a mix or it ranges from
university to university?

00:14:50.366 --> 00:14:52.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That's a great question.

00:14:52.511 --> 00:14:56.160
So, our summer camp is
structured in a way that we have

00:14:56.161 --> 00:14:58.130
a core curriculum that focuses on

00:14:58.131 --> 00:15:00.655
those technical concepts
that we've mentioned.

00:15:00.656 --> 00:15:03.295
Each camp sticks to

00:15:03.296 --> 00:15:06.290
that and tries to keep
that consistent across.

00:15:06.291 --> 00:15:08.210
Then, we have obviously those

00:15:08.211 --> 00:15:10.360
in-person activities
that might vary in

00:15:10.361 --> 00:15:12.785
terms of which individuals

00:15:12.786 --> 00:15:15.055
are on the career
panels and all of that,

00:15:15.056 --> 00:15:17.570
and each university
is responsible for

00:15:17.571 --> 00:15:21.540
developing AI research projects
and partnership with us.

00:15:21.541 --> 00:15:23.185
But that really is based on

00:15:23.186 --> 00:15:25.325
some of the research
being done and out in

00:15:25.326 --> 00:15:26.985
those labs as well as what

00:15:26.986 --> 00:15:30.600
the faculty and PhD students
are really passionate about.

00:15:30.601 --> 00:15:34.150
So, it's very much
taking advantage and

00:15:34.151 --> 00:15:35.690
leveraging the assets that are at

00:15:35.691 --> 00:15:38.060
those universities for
the research projects.

00:15:38.061 --> 00:15:40.700
&gt;&gt; How long is the camp?
Sorry to [inaudible]

00:15:40.701 --> 00:15:43.285
&gt;&gt; Oh no problem. They are
two to three weeks long,

00:15:43.286 --> 00:15:46.390
depending on each side and
they're pretty intensive.

00:15:46.391 --> 00:15:48.135
So, it's all day

00:15:48.136 --> 00:15:50.970
and then they do
fun activities at night.

00:15:50.971 --> 00:15:53.805
So, one of the things
that we learned

00:15:53.806 --> 00:15:55.810
this past year in

00:15:55.811 --> 00:15:58.580
expanding and tripling
the number of camps,

00:15:58.581 --> 00:16:01.110
is that there's
incredibly high demand

00:16:01.111 --> 00:16:02.620
for these types of skills.

00:16:02.621 --> 00:16:05.530
So, one of our program's only has

00:16:05.531 --> 00:16:09.580
20 slots, got 900 applications.

00:16:09.581 --> 00:16:12.100
So, we were like, "Wow,
this is amazing."

00:16:12.101 --> 00:16:15.345
We also get a lot of interest
from all over the world and

00:16:15.346 --> 00:16:17.250
how do we embed AI curriculum

00:16:17.251 --> 00:16:18.850
into what we're already doing,

00:16:18.851 --> 00:16:21.745
places in every
continent basically.

00:16:21.746 --> 00:16:23.790
So, we decided that
we want to open

00:16:23.791 --> 00:16:25.860
source our curriculum
and really try to

00:16:25.861 --> 00:16:28.900
scale the learnings that
we've had and make this

00:16:28.901 --> 00:16:31.420
available worldwide and we see

00:16:31.421 --> 00:16:34.275
that there's tremendous
scale potential here.

00:16:34.276 --> 00:16:35.880
This is very early stage,

00:16:35.881 --> 00:16:38.705
and we look forward to
launching it later this year.

00:16:38.706 --> 00:16:40.850
The third piece of what we do,

00:16:40.851 --> 00:16:43.600
that's very exciting is
taking graduates from

00:16:43.601 --> 00:16:46.380
those first two programs
and connecting them

00:16:46.381 --> 00:16:48.210
with mentors to work on

00:16:48.211 --> 00:16:52.180
more rigorous and
longer-term AI projects.

00:16:52.181 --> 00:16:54.870
Obviously, having portfolios and

00:16:54.871 --> 00:16:56.730
actual demonstrated work product,

00:16:56.731 --> 00:16:57.975
is so critical in

00:16:57.976 --> 00:17:00.470
both your education and
your career these days.

00:17:00.471 --> 00:17:03.945
So, we're really trying to
develop students portfolios.

00:17:03.946 --> 00:17:08.575
So, we just finished
a cohort two weeks ago,

00:17:08.576 --> 00:17:12.470
and we had students working
with mentors from IBM,

00:17:12.471 --> 00:17:15.950
from Pandora, from OpenAI
working on research projects,

00:17:15.951 --> 00:17:17.635
and here are some of them.

00:17:17.636 --> 00:17:20.665
So, using AI to detect wildfires,

00:17:20.666 --> 00:17:25.010
ranking the urgency of
ambulance calls and personalize

00:17:25.011 --> 00:17:27.315
the student on the right
working with a mentor from

00:17:27.316 --> 00:17:29.815
OpenAI built a math tutor,

00:17:29.816 --> 00:17:31.085
that is adaptive and

00:17:31.086 --> 00:17:34.320
personalized that she's
planning to actually launch.

00:17:34.321 --> 00:17:38.260
So, we see incredible
results when we pair

00:17:38.261 --> 00:17:40.600
our young people
with their passions

00:17:40.601 --> 00:17:44.120
and connect them to that support
and that mentorship.

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:46.080
Oh, yes please.

00:17:46.081 --> 00:17:48.600
&gt;&gt; I am wondering for
the mentorship program,

00:17:48.601 --> 00:17:51.060
what is the level
of the students?

00:17:51.061 --> 00:17:52.120
Are they undergraduate or
graduate students, high school?

00:17:52.121 --> 00:17:53.270
&gt;&gt; High school students.

00:17:53.271 --> 00:17:55.535
&gt;&gt; High school students.

00:17:55.536 --> 00:17:59.400
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, they're all
10th through 12th graders.

00:17:59.401 --> 00:18:02.160
So, we talked a little bit about

00:18:02.161 --> 00:18:05.030
AI4ALL's work and why we
think diversity is so

00:18:05.031 --> 00:18:08.475
critical in mitigating
the negative impacts like

00:18:08.476 --> 00:18:11.060
bias and the other ethical risks

00:18:11.061 --> 00:18:12.700
in artificial intelligence.

00:18:12.701 --> 00:18:16.110
But really diversity
is critical also for

00:18:16.111 --> 00:18:18.140
maximizing the potential for

00:18:18.141 --> 00:18:20.480
AI breakthroughs and moonshots.

00:18:20.481 --> 00:18:22.270
So, if we increase

00:18:22.271 --> 00:18:24.920
diversity in AI
these early stages especially,

00:18:24.921 --> 00:18:27.225
we're going to see
more innovative products,

00:18:27.226 --> 00:18:30.300
a more diverse set of
problems actually addressed,

00:18:30.301 --> 00:18:31.810
as well as the network

00:18:31.811 --> 00:18:34.030
effect of bringing
more people into the field.

00:18:34.031 --> 00:18:37.945
I want to share several case
studies that showcase this.

00:18:37.946 --> 00:18:40.200
So, we know that

00:18:40.201 --> 00:18:42.975
diversity is just generally
good for business.

00:18:42.976 --> 00:18:44.980
Recent Intel report
showed that if we

00:18:44.981 --> 00:18:47.210
increase diversity
in the tech economy,

00:18:47.211 --> 00:18:52.310
it could add $500 billion
to national GDP.

00:18:52.311 --> 00:18:55.640
So, it's a good
business proposition.

00:18:55.641 --> 00:18:58.030
Additionally,we'll see greater

00:18:58.031 --> 00:19:00.545
innovation from
increasing diversity.

00:19:00.546 --> 00:19:04.845
So, recent study from
Raj Chetty showed that if we

00:19:04.846 --> 00:19:09.195
increase early exposure
to innovation,

00:19:09.196 --> 00:19:11.450
the innovation economy to

00:19:11.451 --> 00:19:14.110
women minorities and
low-income students,

00:19:14.111 --> 00:19:17.715
the rate of innovation in
America would quadruple,

00:19:17.716 --> 00:19:19.640
this is no small thing.

00:19:19.641 --> 00:19:23.460
So, it's really important to
have that early exposure,

00:19:23.461 --> 00:19:28.125
so that we can maximize
innovation in our economy.

00:19:28.126 --> 00:19:30.460
The second thing is we might miss

00:19:30.461 --> 00:19:32.560
out on untapped talent that

00:19:32.561 --> 00:19:36.465
might develop the next big thing
in AI or otherwise.

00:19:36.466 --> 00:19:38.495
I'll share the example of Amy.

00:19:38.496 --> 00:19:40.010
So, Amy graduated from

00:19:40.011 --> 00:19:43.980
our Stanford AI4ALL
program in 2015.

00:19:43.981 --> 00:19:46.500
She was so passionate about AI.

00:19:46.501 --> 00:19:47.980
She had never have
been exposed to it

00:19:47.981 --> 00:19:51.345
before that she kept
on with her research.

00:19:51.346 --> 00:19:52.780
How many of you have heard of

00:19:52.781 --> 00:19:56.205
the NIPS Conference?
Okay, everyone.

00:19:56.206 --> 00:20:02.100
So, Amy won Best Paper Award
going up against hundreds of

00:20:02.101 --> 00:20:04.420
adults for her research on

00:20:04.421 --> 00:20:08.250
improving surgeon technique
using machine learning.

00:20:08.251 --> 00:20:09.690
This is pretty amazing for

00:20:09.691 --> 00:20:11.560
a student that's
still in high school.

00:20:11.561 --> 00:20:13.795
So, we don't want to miss out on

00:20:13.796 --> 00:20:17.140
talent like Amy that might
develop the next big thing.

00:20:17.141 --> 00:20:20.520
If Amy had never heard of AI
or never been exposed to it,

00:20:20.521 --> 00:20:22.865
she never would have
had that opportunity.

00:20:22.866 --> 00:20:26.790
We also see that if we increase
inclusion and diversity,

00:20:26.791 --> 00:20:29.270
a more diverse set of

00:20:29.271 --> 00:20:30.710
problems more creative set of

00:20:30.711 --> 00:20:32.595
problems will actually
be addressed.

00:20:32.596 --> 00:20:35.225
So, I want to share
the story of Stephanie.

00:20:35.226 --> 00:20:37.220
So, Stephanie is from Salinas,

00:20:37.221 --> 00:20:38.700
California, not sure if

00:20:38.701 --> 00:20:41.005
any of you are
familiar with Salinas.

00:20:41.006 --> 00:20:45.780
But it is an area where
she actually grew up as

00:20:45.781 --> 00:20:47.370
a daughter of farm workers in

00:20:47.371 --> 00:20:48.845
a very low income family,

00:20:48.846 --> 00:20:51.120
first generation
Mexican-American.

00:20:51.121 --> 00:20:53.555
She graduated from our program.

00:20:53.556 --> 00:20:57.090
Not only did she start
an AI club at her high school,

00:20:57.091 --> 00:20:58.270
which is pretty impressive,

00:20:58.271 --> 00:21:02.435
and she's teaching
younger students about AI.

00:21:02.436 --> 00:21:06.200
She's doing research
to track the flow of

00:21:06.201 --> 00:21:07.870
contaminated water using machine

00:21:07.871 --> 00:21:10.205
learning with her
mentor from Accenture.

00:21:10.206 --> 00:21:12.130
This is something
that she's still in

00:21:12.131 --> 00:21:14.050
the early stages of
but she wants to bring

00:21:14.051 --> 00:21:15.230
this back to her community

00:21:15.231 --> 00:21:16.650
because it's something
that's faced

00:21:16.651 --> 00:21:20.955
her both personally and directly.

00:21:20.956 --> 00:21:23.430
So, here's Bekah.

00:21:23.431 --> 00:21:26.325
So, Bekah went through
our AI4ALL program,

00:21:26.326 --> 00:21:29.490
and she was really torn

00:21:29.491 --> 00:21:32.140
between her passion for

00:21:32.141 --> 00:21:34.940
social justice and
humanitarian work.

00:21:34.941 --> 00:21:38.250
But through exposing her to
all the different ways that

00:21:38.251 --> 00:21:41.794
AI can be applied for good
and to benefit humanity,

00:21:41.795 --> 00:21:43.720
she became really
really passionate

00:21:43.721 --> 00:21:46.020
about combining
those two pieces together.

00:21:46.021 --> 00:21:47.530
So I'll just share a quote.

00:21:47.531 --> 00:21:49.670
Seeing the humanitarian
applications

00:21:49.671 --> 00:21:51.730
in AI at Stanford AI4ALL,

00:21:51.731 --> 00:21:54.215
I realized that I didn't
have to sacrifice

00:21:54.216 --> 00:21:56.770
fundamental aspects
of my identity

00:21:56.771 --> 00:21:58.610
to pursue computer science.

00:21:58.611 --> 00:22:00.930
I love computer science
and I see it as

00:22:00.931 --> 00:22:03.230
a tool to utilize in art,

00:22:03.231 --> 00:22:05.550
music, and political advocacy.

00:22:05.551 --> 00:22:09.535
STEM can be really powerful
when applied to other fields.

00:22:09.536 --> 00:22:12.980
So, we see that if we
increase diversity,

00:22:12.981 --> 00:22:16.105
we're not only going to get
more creative uses of AI,

00:22:16.106 --> 00:22:18.210
discover untapped talent that

00:22:18.211 --> 00:22:19.815
we wouldn't have found before,

00:22:19.816 --> 00:22:23.260
but also new interdisciplinary
ways to apply AI to

00:22:23.261 --> 00:22:25.840
different fields
and really tap into

00:22:25.841 --> 00:22:29.245
people's unique passion
and interests.

00:22:29.246 --> 00:22:31.450
We also see that when we

00:22:31.451 --> 00:22:33.270
increase diversity and inclusion,

00:22:33.271 --> 00:22:35.470
it really has
this network effect that

00:22:35.471 --> 00:22:38.075
we've seen that's been
really, really powerful.

00:22:38.076 --> 00:22:40.935
So, most of our students leave

00:22:40.936 --> 00:22:44.220
AI4ALL programs wanting to pursue

00:22:44.221 --> 00:22:45.590
AI and feeling like they're

00:22:45.591 --> 00:22:47.705
part of this
incredible community.

00:22:47.706 --> 00:22:51.095
What that's led to is that
they go on to educate

00:22:51.096 --> 00:22:54.905
their peers as well as their
community and we track this.

00:22:54.906 --> 00:22:57.715
So, for every one student
that we educate,

00:22:57.716 --> 00:22:59.990
they go on to educate 11 more.

00:22:59.991 --> 00:23:02.655
So, when you bring
more people into the field,

00:23:02.656 --> 00:23:05.635
it has this incredible
multiplier effect.

00:23:05.636 --> 00:23:06.720
They bring their friends,

00:23:06.721 --> 00:23:09.790
they bring their friends
and just a small investment

00:23:09.791 --> 00:23:11.100
in one person can be really

00:23:11.101 --> 00:23:13.320
powerful in making
an impact there.

00:23:13.321 --> 00:23:15.140
We also see the same thing

00:23:15.141 --> 00:23:17.455
with organizations and companies.

00:23:17.456 --> 00:23:20.240
I think Microsoft is
certainly a great example

00:23:20.241 --> 00:23:22.910
of this where when
one company steps up

00:23:22.911 --> 00:23:29.990
and invests in really
this principled approach

00:23:29.991 --> 00:23:32.450
to AI development
and really looking

00:23:32.451 --> 00:23:36.540
at bias and mitigating that
at these earliest stages,

00:23:36.541 --> 00:23:39.090
as well as investing in
the next generation of talent,

00:23:39.091 --> 00:23:42.350
it then brings other
leaders into the field and

00:23:42.351 --> 00:23:46.055
it shows the way and it
influences other companies.

00:23:46.056 --> 00:23:47.730
So, these are a lot of

00:23:47.731 --> 00:23:49.400
our AI4ALL partners that we

00:23:49.401 --> 00:23:51.820
currently work with and
the list is growing.

00:23:51.821 --> 00:23:54.200
So, I think it's really
important not just

00:23:54.201 --> 00:23:56.630
to have inclusion and

00:23:56.631 --> 00:24:01.530
diversity bringing in
individuals into the field,

00:24:01.531 --> 00:24:02.750
but also in showing

00:24:02.751 --> 00:24:06.280
other companies and
organizations and institutions

00:24:06.281 --> 00:24:08.770
that really need
to step up and be

00:24:08.771 --> 00:24:10.090
leaders in this way and bring

00:24:10.091 --> 00:24:12.840
their colleagues and peers along.

00:24:12.841 --> 00:24:15.860
So, I want to talk a little bit

00:24:15.861 --> 00:24:19.955
about how you all can
get involved with us.

00:24:19.956 --> 00:24:22.180
So, we currently
are really excited

00:24:22.181 --> 00:24:24.370
about our partnership
with Microsoft.

00:24:24.371 --> 00:24:28.990
There's a couple of specific
ways that I've set out

00:24:28.991 --> 00:24:30.970
here that we'd love to

00:24:30.971 --> 00:24:33.705
continue deepening this work
that we're doing together.

00:24:33.706 --> 00:24:35.230
But I also want to hear from

00:24:35.231 --> 00:24:36.975
you all if there
are other things.

00:24:36.976 --> 00:24:38.640
The first is recruiting.

00:24:38.641 --> 00:24:41.315
So, we are obviously graduating

00:24:41.316 --> 00:24:46.370
an amazing set of the next
generation of AI leaders.

00:24:46.371 --> 00:24:48.950
So, if you have internships,

00:24:48.951 --> 00:24:50.925
if you have an
apprenticeship program,

00:24:50.926 --> 00:24:53.065
if you're hiring, consider

00:24:53.066 --> 00:24:57.620
AI4ALL a source of
talent for these roles.

00:24:57.621 --> 00:24:59.680
Secondly, we have many,

00:24:59.681 --> 00:25:02.470
many volunteering opportunities
for folks to serve

00:25:02.471 --> 00:25:05.995
as mentors for this next
generation of talent.

00:25:05.996 --> 00:25:09.410
A mentor can absolutely
change someone's life and

00:25:09.411 --> 00:25:12.310
change someone's
trajectory and we have

00:25:12.311 --> 00:25:15.000
both one-day
opportunities as well as

00:25:15.001 --> 00:25:17.020
these longer-term
opportunities to support

00:25:17.021 --> 00:25:19.805
students on an actual
research project.

00:25:19.806 --> 00:25:22.850
Share feedback, again that's
such an important part.

00:25:22.851 --> 00:25:24.870
We consider this a dialogue and

00:25:24.871 --> 00:25:27.020
a two-way street that
we want to learn

00:25:27.021 --> 00:25:28.660
what are you all finding and

00:25:28.661 --> 00:25:31.925
the best practices
for mitigating bias.

00:25:31.926 --> 00:25:33.300
What are some of
the challenges and

00:25:33.301 --> 00:25:35.400
nuances that you're
seeing in your work and

00:25:35.401 --> 00:25:38.810
how can we leverage
those learnings to prepare

00:25:38.811 --> 00:25:40.700
the next generation to do

00:25:40.701 --> 00:25:42.960
this well and to make
the right decisions?

00:25:42.961 --> 00:25:46.575
So, we want to hear from you
and worked closely on that.

00:25:46.576 --> 00:25:48.760
I know you all also do
a lot of outreach and

00:25:48.761 --> 00:25:50.100
education yourselves and some

00:25:50.101 --> 00:25:51.650
of you in the room
are doing that.

00:25:51.651 --> 00:25:53.420
I want to hear more,
what's working,

00:25:53.421 --> 00:25:57.410
how can we partner and
collaborate on those efforts.

00:25:57.411 --> 00:26:00.040
This problem is
obviously not something

00:26:00.041 --> 00:26:02.830
that one organization or
one company can solve,

00:26:02.831 --> 00:26:04.370
so it's really important
for us to come

00:26:04.371 --> 00:26:07.945
together and really
solve this together.

00:26:07.946 --> 00:26:12.015
So with that, and if you are
interested in volunteering,

00:26:12.016 --> 00:26:13.690
I have a link right here

00:26:13.691 --> 00:26:15.360
that makes it really
easy to sign up and

00:26:15.361 --> 00:26:18.275
you can let us know if
you're interested and

00:26:18.276 --> 00:26:21.660
in what capacity and also
our contact information.

00:26:21.661 --> 00:26:23.760
But I'd love to hear from
the room if you have

00:26:23.761 --> 00:26:26.220
questions either
about our work or

00:26:26.221 --> 00:26:29.925
ideas for ways that we
can work together to

00:26:29.926 --> 00:26:34.230
both support the next generation
of diverse AI leaders,

00:26:34.231 --> 00:26:37.880
but also ensure
a responsible and ethical use

00:26:37.881 --> 00:26:41.110
and deployment of
this important technology.

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:47.615
So, any questions
thoughts feedback? Yes.

00:26:47.616 --> 00:26:49.190
&gt;&gt; Okay. I apologize,

00:26:49.191 --> 00:26:51.105
I was a few minutes late
coming from another meeting.

00:26:51.106 --> 00:26:51.510
&gt;&gt; No problem.

00:26:51.511 --> 00:26:54.470
&gt;&gt; Let's me show number
of students touched,

00:26:54.471 --> 00:26:56.325
which is extremely impressive.

00:26:56.326 --> 00:27:00.860
How are you getting
that volume of touch,

00:27:00.861 --> 00:27:04.760
when you say in programs that
only accept 20 students?

00:27:04.761 --> 00:27:06.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:27:06.141 --> 00:27:07.190
&gt;&gt; So, where does
that number came from?

00:27:07.191 --> 00:27:09.895
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, right now,

00:27:09.896 --> 00:27:12.120
our main programs are at

00:27:12.121 --> 00:27:15.815
these universities that have
between 20 and 30 slots.

00:27:15.816 --> 00:27:17.720
I think what you're
referring to is

00:27:17.721 --> 00:27:19.710
our open sourcing project

00:27:19.711 --> 00:27:22.560
that is really our scaling plans.

00:27:22.561 --> 00:27:26.455
So, we're getting so much demand
and so much interest.

00:27:26.456 --> 00:27:32.040
We believe that this access
to AI tools, education,

00:27:32.041 --> 00:27:35.160
and resources should
be widely accessible,

00:27:35.161 --> 00:27:37.355
and that's not getting
addressed right now,

00:27:37.356 --> 00:27:39.845
at least in the US
at the K-12 level.

00:27:39.846 --> 00:27:41.750
So, we're taking the curriculum

00:27:41.751 --> 00:27:43.070
and learning that we've built for

00:27:43.071 --> 00:27:44.570
our summer camps and open

00:27:44.571 --> 00:27:46.855
sourcing them to make them
more widely available.

00:27:46.856 --> 00:27:48.260
Which we have the goal of

00:27:48.261 --> 00:27:50.570
reaching millions
through that model,

00:27:50.571 --> 00:27:53.455
by working with
existing partners, schools,

00:27:53.456 --> 00:27:57.225
and companies who are
already doing this work.

00:27:57.226 --> 00:27:59.450
Thank you, good question.

00:27:59.451 --> 00:28:02.020
&gt;&gt; So, when your summer
camp curriculum,

00:28:02.021 --> 00:28:03.870
what are the kinds
of applications

00:28:03.871 --> 00:28:05.540
you got the kids working on?

00:28:05.541 --> 00:28:09.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, we
focus on robotics,

00:28:09.691 --> 00:28:12.390
computer vision,
natural language processing,

00:28:12.391 --> 00:28:14.780
and each of the
universities develops

00:28:14.781 --> 00:28:16.990
a set of projects that are

00:28:16.991 --> 00:28:19.100
focused on those applications

00:28:19.101 --> 00:28:21.520
but are all around AI for good.

00:28:21.521 --> 00:28:24.520
So, for example, looking
at social media data

00:28:24.521 --> 00:28:28.190
and analyzing that after
a natural disaster,

00:28:28.191 --> 00:28:30.770
we're looking at
after Hurricane Sandy

00:28:30.771 --> 00:28:33.630
dataset to see how
would you better funnel

00:28:33.631 --> 00:28:36.890
resources towards that
in response to sort

00:28:36.891 --> 00:28:41.185
of lessen response times and
make them more accurate,

00:28:41.186 --> 00:28:45.080
making hospitals safer
using computer vision,

00:28:45.081 --> 00:28:48.280
or how does the
self-driving car revolution

00:28:48.281 --> 00:28:52.045
affect mobility for
ageing populations.

00:28:52.046 --> 00:28:54.260
So, those are some
of them and we have

00:28:54.261 --> 00:28:56.440
about four to five
projects at each of

00:28:56.441 --> 00:28:59.565
our universities
that are co-designed

00:28:59.566 --> 00:29:03.160
with those researchers and
faculty. Great question.

00:29:03.161 --> 00:29:05.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love that,
because when I look at

00:29:05.411 --> 00:29:08.060
the most curricula
especially for kids,

00:29:08.061 --> 00:29:09.860
you see sort of
the same things come

00:29:09.861 --> 00:29:12.355
up again and again like robots.

00:29:12.356 --> 00:29:15.785
A kid likes, like
the one you showed.

00:29:15.786 --> 00:29:17.560
It was really interested
in social justice

00:29:17.561 --> 00:29:18.835
that's just not
going to turn off.

00:29:18.836 --> 00:29:21.085
So, I love the approach
you're taking.

00:29:21.086 --> 00:29:23.080
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Yes?

00:29:23.081 --> 00:29:26.660
&gt;&gt; What about datasets for these.

00:29:26.661 --> 00:29:30.110
Isn't that the program
has dataset at

00:29:30.111 --> 00:29:32.660
all universities or
other researchers

00:29:32.661 --> 00:29:35.765
at different institutions
providing the datasets?

00:29:35.766 --> 00:29:39.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Great question.
It's a combination.

00:29:39.271 --> 00:29:41.180
Obviously getting good datasets

00:29:41.181 --> 00:29:42.950
is one of the key
challenges, right?

00:29:42.951 --> 00:29:46.370
So, that's part of what we
try to teach our students is,

00:29:46.371 --> 00:29:48.340
"How do you find
the right dataset?

00:29:48.341 --> 00:29:50.240
How do you analyze the dataset?"

00:29:50.241 --> 00:29:53.980
So, it's quality, what
is the size matter,

00:29:53.981 --> 00:29:55.400
where do you host it all that.

00:29:55.401 --> 00:29:58.345
So, that's why we certainly
provide some of them.

00:29:58.346 --> 00:30:01.025
We also allow the students
to try to go out and

00:30:01.026 --> 00:30:04.740
identify where can you find
this kind of open source.

00:30:04.741 --> 00:30:06.230
We've also leverage tools like

00:30:06.231 --> 00:30:09.140
Kaggle and other kind

00:30:09.141 --> 00:30:10.680
of open-source tools
that are out there,

00:30:10.681 --> 00:30:12.250
and in some cases
the universities

00:30:12.251 --> 00:30:14.075
will provide those datasets.

00:30:14.076 --> 00:30:17.615
It's a great question. Yes?

00:30:17.616 --> 00:30:21.005
&gt;&gt; Do you also have
opportunities for people who are

00:30:21.006 --> 00:30:23.030
already sort of studied the field

00:30:23.031 --> 00:30:25.250
and sort of looking
to move into that?

00:30:25.251 --> 00:30:28.580
Because these are I think
mostly focus in students,

00:30:28.581 --> 00:30:29.930
highschool students who are

00:30:29.931 --> 00:30:31.570
interested in getting
into it and they

00:30:31.571 --> 00:30:35.330
haven't had no education
shaped in a particular way,

00:30:35.331 --> 00:30:36.360
but do you also have
opportunities for

00:30:36.361 --> 00:30:37.700
people switching fields?

00:30:37.701 --> 00:30:39.190
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:30:39.191 --> 00:30:40.880
So, currently we don't.

00:30:40.881 --> 00:30:44.520
So our summer camps are
mainly for highschoolers,

00:30:44.521 --> 00:30:46.740
but our open source
curriculum will

00:30:46.741 --> 00:30:49.125
be available and open
for anyone to use,

00:30:49.126 --> 00:30:51.240
because obviously
there is a gap in

00:30:51.241 --> 00:30:54.670
basic AI training at all levels.

00:30:54.671 --> 00:30:56.645
So, it's good question.

00:30:56.646 --> 00:30:59.015
Other questions, yes?

00:30:59.016 --> 00:31:00.500
&gt;&gt; I think there's a difference

00:31:00.501 --> 00:31:03.320
between creating wagon adding

00:31:03.321 --> 00:31:07.925
summer course and the
approach summer schools.

00:31:07.926 --> 00:31:10.375
So, I feel like

00:31:10.376 --> 00:31:13.435
the impact you're having
you can actually access it,

00:31:13.436 --> 00:31:15.910
if the students are
sitting at home and just

00:31:15.911 --> 00:31:18.660
watching the material
in an online ways.

00:31:18.661 --> 00:31:19.940
There seems like there's

00:31:19.941 --> 00:31:23.000
a human touch parts and
meeting these people,

00:31:23.001 --> 00:31:25.625
working with them, and
getting their hands dirty.

00:31:25.626 --> 00:31:28.490
So, in terms of scaling that,

00:31:28.491 --> 00:31:30.620
open sourcing the contact is

00:31:30.621 --> 00:31:32.435
one part of it but I
guess you still need

00:31:32.436 --> 00:31:34.520
partners in different parts
of the country

00:31:34.521 --> 00:31:36.635
and the world that are going to

00:31:36.636 --> 00:31:40.880
physically have host
these camps and be the mentors.

00:31:40.881 --> 00:31:44.060
I'm trying to understand
how much of it is going

00:31:44.061 --> 00:31:47.435
to be the open-source content
and that scales?

00:31:47.436 --> 00:31:49.235
How much of it is
still going to be

00:31:49.236 --> 00:31:52.025
the organizational and hands on

00:31:52.026 --> 00:31:55.850
and one-to-one mentoring
kind of style.

00:31:55.851 --> 00:31:57.615
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah.

00:31:57.616 --> 00:31:59.610
No, it's a good
question and we find

00:31:59.611 --> 00:32:02.325
obviously a lot of
the barriers for

00:32:02.326 --> 00:32:04.510
people that are
currently excluded from

00:32:04.511 --> 00:32:07.560
these fields have to do
with lack of role models,

00:32:07.561 --> 00:32:09.060
and mentors, and those pieces,

00:32:09.061 --> 00:32:12.465
and we don't want to miss
that in these new models.

00:32:12.466 --> 00:32:14.780
So, in terms of
our open-sourcing curriculum,

00:32:14.781 --> 00:32:17.125
it's not going to be just a MOOC.

00:32:17.126 --> 00:32:19.070
So, there will be some materials

00:32:19.071 --> 00:32:21.040
that will be openly available,

00:32:21.041 --> 00:32:23.290
but then we also are working with

00:32:23.291 --> 00:32:25.780
partners closely who are already

00:32:25.781 --> 00:32:30.045
teaching let's say
CS classes or run

00:32:30.046 --> 00:32:32.700
after school STEM programs

00:32:32.701 --> 00:32:35.715
that can actually provide
that in-person component.

00:32:35.716 --> 00:32:38.060
So, absolutely we
don't want to miss

00:32:38.061 --> 00:32:40.200
out on that because
the peer community,

00:32:40.201 --> 00:32:41.935
that human touch as you said,

00:32:41.936 --> 00:32:43.590
is really a critical part of

00:32:43.591 --> 00:32:46.520
why our programs have
been successful.

00:32:46.521 --> 00:32:48.180
We also will be offering

00:32:48.181 --> 00:32:49.930
again that mentorship,
fellowship,

00:32:49.931 --> 00:32:51.700
opportunity to students that

00:32:51.701 --> 00:32:53.340
want to continue their learning.

00:32:53.341 --> 00:32:55.900
We also see that with
the open source piece,

00:32:55.901 --> 00:32:57.160
that will really give people

00:32:57.161 --> 00:33:00.750
exposure to what is
available in this field,

00:33:00.751 --> 00:33:04.210
and then they can take
that knowledge further.

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:07.810
I think there's a big gap
right now in terms

00:33:07.811 --> 00:33:10.685
of what is even a career in AI.

00:33:10.686 --> 00:33:12.530
So, the work that you all do

00:33:12.531 --> 00:33:15.075
is absolutely so interesting,

00:33:15.076 --> 00:33:16.945
and if more people could

00:33:16.946 --> 00:33:19.010
understand like what are
the different pathways,

00:33:19.011 --> 00:33:21.005
what are the things
that you can work on,

00:33:21.006 --> 00:33:24.590
I think that will inspire a lot
of people to pursue this.

00:33:24.591 --> 00:33:26.045
So, we're also going to have

00:33:26.046 --> 00:33:28.630
a career exploration, component,

00:33:28.631 --> 00:33:30.850
and element where
people know, hey,

00:33:30.851 --> 00:33:32.070
if I'm really interested in

00:33:32.071 --> 00:33:33.910
research and what does
that actually look like,

00:33:33.911 --> 00:33:36.330
I can go in this pathway
in this direction.

00:33:36.331 --> 00:33:39.660
So, I think that will give
them specific steps of how

00:33:39.661 --> 00:33:43.240
to move on to that next phase
of their learning.

00:33:43.241 --> 00:33:47.210
Good question. Other thoughts?

00:33:47.211 --> 00:33:51.950
&gt;&gt; I think in terms of
other side of career in AI,

00:33:51.951 --> 00:33:54.305
it seems like the program covers

00:33:54.306 --> 00:33:58.090
the education part and
the research archivist partners,

00:33:58.091 --> 00:34:00.570
but it seems to me
like there is a gap

00:34:00.571 --> 00:34:03.400
like the industrial part
is missing here,

00:34:03.401 --> 00:34:06.740
because AI is more like

00:34:06.741 --> 00:34:08.690
a common practice
or will become more

00:34:08.691 --> 00:34:10.730
common in the industry part,

00:34:10.731 --> 00:34:13.550
with engineering segment as

00:34:13.551 --> 00:34:16.265
well as data scientist
and analyst.

00:34:16.266 --> 00:34:19.130
These parties are
missing this program.

00:34:19.131 --> 00:34:22.470
So, is there a specific reason
why you focus just to

00:34:22.471 --> 00:34:26.365
focus on research instead
of the engineering part?

00:34:26.366 --> 00:34:28.200
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:34:28.201 --> 00:34:29.630
So, I think that's where

00:34:29.631 --> 00:34:31.360
our industry partners come

00:34:31.361 --> 00:34:33.135
in and are so critical to this.

00:34:33.136 --> 00:34:35.670
So, we're actually
working with a lot of

00:34:35.671 --> 00:34:38.335
different AI focus companies

00:34:38.336 --> 00:34:39.950
that can offer our students

00:34:39.951 --> 00:34:42.105
both internship opportunities,

00:34:42.106 --> 00:34:43.950
job shadow opportunities,

00:34:43.951 --> 00:34:45.580
mentor opportunities
to really get

00:34:45.581 --> 00:34:49.220
exposure to what
these different careers

00:34:49.221 --> 00:34:51.020
are not just in research.

00:34:51.021 --> 00:34:52.980
We focused on research in

00:34:52.981 --> 00:34:54.750
the class itself because
that's a fun and

00:34:54.751 --> 00:34:56.230
hands-on way for students to get

00:34:56.231 --> 00:34:58.405
exposure to some of
the applications.

00:34:58.406 --> 00:35:01.625
We also do cover
some Python coding,

00:35:01.626 --> 00:35:03.900
but they're also when you look at

00:35:03.901 --> 00:35:05.910
the computer science
education world,

00:35:05.911 --> 00:35:08.360
they're actually a lot
of organizations that

00:35:08.361 --> 00:35:11.075
are doing really amazing
work in teaching,

00:35:11.076 --> 00:35:13.660
coding, and engineering
in computer science.

00:35:13.661 --> 00:35:15.690
So, we're not trying
to duplicate that,

00:35:15.691 --> 00:35:17.735
we're actually trying
to be complimentary.

00:35:17.736 --> 00:35:20.130
So, if somebody wants
to continue and

00:35:20.131 --> 00:35:22.490
deepen their Python skills,

00:35:22.491 --> 00:35:24.370
we provide them with
resources but we're

00:35:24.371 --> 00:35:26.960
really not coding program.

00:35:26.961 --> 00:35:30.280
So, both were trying to
provide that exposure to

00:35:30.281 --> 00:35:33.560
industry through our partners
and connecting students.

00:35:33.561 --> 00:35:36.280
So, at each camp we'll have

00:35:36.281 --> 00:35:38.625
many guest speakers from

00:35:38.626 --> 00:35:41.780
industry talking about
their work, their applications.

00:35:41.781 --> 00:35:44.765
We have career round tables
and mentorship opportunities.

00:35:44.766 --> 00:35:45.910
So, they are still getting

00:35:45.911 --> 00:35:48.085
that exposure to
the different sides of it.

00:35:48.086 --> 00:35:49.520
We're not trying to say you

00:35:49.521 --> 00:35:51.300
should go into one or the other,

00:35:51.301 --> 00:35:54.550
it's not really
a directive experience.

00:35:54.551 --> 00:35:57.470
It's really like here are
the broad opportunities

00:35:57.471 --> 00:36:00.670
and you need to select which
path is right for you,

00:36:00.671 --> 00:36:02.040
and here's what you need to be

00:36:02.041 --> 00:36:04.210
successful in that path
and we'll support you.

00:36:04.211 --> 00:36:06.230
But we're certainly agnostic

00:36:06.231 --> 00:36:09.005
in terms of what
the opportunities actually are.

00:36:09.006 --> 00:36:11.360
Great question. Yes?

00:36:11.361 --> 00:36:13.230
&gt;&gt; This is really amazing work.

00:36:13.231 --> 00:36:14.560
Thank you for sharing with us.

00:36:14.561 --> 00:36:16.000
I have the question about

00:36:16.001 --> 00:36:19.360
the innovation beyond
the education piece.

00:36:19.361 --> 00:36:20.660
The innovation
that's coming out of

00:36:20.661 --> 00:36:23.105
the projects that some of
these students are doing.

00:36:23.106 --> 00:36:25.790
What mechanisms do you have
in based on what children

00:36:25.791 --> 00:36:29.670
sustain or provide
some sort of path forward,

00:36:29.671 --> 00:36:32.110
the example that you gave,

00:36:32.111 --> 00:36:34.550
the contaminate water flow.

00:36:34.551 --> 00:36:36.800
So, what falling mechanisms

00:36:36.801 --> 00:36:38.465
have you put in place to do that?

00:36:38.466 --> 00:36:39.850
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:36:39.851 --> 00:36:41.490
So, we're actually expanding out

00:36:41.491 --> 00:36:43.440
our alumni program right now with

00:36:43.441 --> 00:36:45.885
that exact goal in mind.

00:36:45.886 --> 00:36:47.940
We found that students,

00:36:47.941 --> 00:36:51.345
I think it was pleasantly
surprising to us that

00:36:51.346 --> 00:36:52.960
a lot of students
wanted to continue

00:36:52.961 --> 00:36:55.065
that research already
in high school.

00:36:55.066 --> 00:36:58.190
So, we are putting
more formal mechanisms

00:36:58.191 --> 00:36:59.530
into place as well as keeping

00:36:59.531 --> 00:37:01.320
that community element going.

00:37:01.321 --> 00:37:04.495
So, we have ongoing
educational opportunities.

00:37:04.496 --> 00:37:06.850
We are now starting to

00:37:06.851 --> 00:37:09.260
