diverse as well as this
open source piece,

00:49:27.306 --> 00:49:30.420
because access is such
a huge, huge issue.

00:49:30.421 --> 00:49:33.020
I think though one of the
challenges I've worked

00:49:33.021 --> 00:49:35.450
in rural areas in the past,

00:49:35.451 --> 00:49:37.985
and bandwidth and
Internet connectivity,

00:49:37.986 --> 00:49:40.270
50 percent of
low-income households in

00:49:40.271 --> 00:49:42.660
the U.S. don't have access
to internet at home,

00:49:42.661 --> 00:49:45.460
which is shocking
to a lot of people,

00:49:45.461 --> 00:49:48.405
but even if you go
to a local library,

00:49:48.406 --> 00:49:50.350
the bandwidth may not
be good enough to

00:49:50.351 --> 00:49:52.135
even run a YouTube video.

00:49:52.136 --> 00:49:53.670
So, how do we address
that and more

00:49:53.671 --> 00:49:55.240
of an infrastructure level?

00:49:55.241 --> 00:49:57.430
So that we can really
provide access to

00:49:57.431 --> 00:49:58.800
rural areas that are

00:49:58.801 --> 00:50:01.610
really left behind in
the digital divide.

00:50:01.611 --> 00:50:04.590
&gt;&gt; The second question
is we keep hearing

00:50:04.591 --> 00:50:08.010
about an AI ethical
framework and principles,

00:50:08.011 --> 00:50:11.010
do you have a draft
on those principles?

00:50:11.011 --> 00:50:13.230
What is the road map for having

00:50:13.231 --> 00:50:16.335
a broader agreement on the
framework and principles?

00:50:16.336 --> 00:50:18.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That's a great question.

00:50:18.181 --> 00:50:20.060
So, we are not

00:50:20.061 --> 00:50:23.975
a framework development
organization ourselves.

00:50:23.976 --> 00:50:26.860
The best that we
can do right now is

00:50:26.861 --> 00:50:29.430
really stay connected to

00:50:29.431 --> 00:50:32.130
the conversations of who is
developing those frameworks.

00:50:32.131 --> 00:50:35.755
So, for example, like
I mentioned iTripoli,

00:50:35.756 --> 00:50:39.885
their AI and Autonomous
Systems Ethics Initiative,

00:50:39.886 --> 00:50:41.290
it's a long name,

00:50:41.291 --> 00:50:44.520
has great work being done on
creating those standards.

00:50:44.521 --> 00:50:45.900
They actually just put out

00:50:45.901 --> 00:50:48.430
a paper called,
Ethically Aligned Design,

00:50:48.431 --> 00:50:50.390
that has a set of
principles that they

00:50:50.391 --> 00:50:52.425
put forth that were developed in

00:50:52.426 --> 00:50:56.339
these working groups
from very diverse areas,

00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:58.960
and I definitely recommend
taking a look at that.

00:50:58.961 --> 00:51:03.255
It's a really interesting
and in-depth-

00:51:03.256 --> 00:51:05.370
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] ethics,
committee and framework.

00:51:05.371 --> 00:51:06.030
&gt;&gt; Sorry, what?

00:51:06.031 --> 00:51:07.050
&gt;&gt; ACM, [inaudible].

00:51:07.051 --> 00:51:10.690
&gt;&gt; ACM. Yes. Exactly.
There's a group called

00:51:10.691 --> 00:51:15.645
Partnerships on AI that
Microsoft is also involved with,

00:51:15.646 --> 00:51:17.130
that is working on creating

00:51:17.131 --> 00:51:19.570
working groups to create
standards as well.

00:51:19.571 --> 00:51:21.600
So, I think there's a lot
of these initiatives

00:51:21.601 --> 00:51:23.940
that we're seeing happening,

00:51:23.941 --> 00:51:25.310
and we're not
putting forth one of

00:51:25.311 --> 00:51:27.580
these kind of set of standards,

00:51:27.581 --> 00:51:29.470
but rather exposing
our students to

00:51:29.471 --> 00:51:31.990
these different initiatives
that are going on,

00:51:31.991 --> 00:51:36.070
and what some of the nuances
in the discussions are.

00:51:36.071 --> 00:51:38.370
So, I hope that answers

00:51:38.371 --> 00:51:42.270
the question.
Any other questions?

00:51:42.271 --> 00:51:45.465
&gt;&gt; I have a broader
question, which is,

00:51:45.466 --> 00:51:50.490
last year there was
the Google employee,

00:51:50.491 --> 00:51:53.040
there was this memo from
a Google employee talking

00:51:53.041 --> 00:51:55.500
about overall woman in the field,

00:51:55.501 --> 00:51:58.890
and how there's positive
discrimination and

00:51:58.891 --> 00:52:03.480
men are actually first off
because of that, and so forth.

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:07.425
Being in this community,

00:52:07.426 --> 00:52:11.040
how do you think we are
reacting towards these kind

00:52:11.041 --> 00:52:17.605
of backlashes or how do
we change the culture?

00:52:17.606 --> 00:52:19.440
I'm just curious
about your thoughts

00:52:19.441 --> 00:52:21.855
about all of this discussion

00:52:21.856 --> 00:52:23.340
that's going on in this space,

00:52:23.341 --> 00:52:25.050
not so much about
high school students,

00:52:25.051 --> 00:52:27.585
but about the industry
that we're in.

00:52:27.586 --> 00:52:29.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah. No, it's a really,

00:52:29.421 --> 00:52:31.550
really great question and I

00:52:31.551 --> 00:52:34.640
think there's been a lot
of attention on it lately,

00:52:34.641 --> 00:52:36.620
with the Me Too movement and

00:52:36.621 --> 00:52:39.575
certainly that memo that
you're referring to.

00:52:39.576 --> 00:52:42.640
I think as more and
more people speak out,

00:52:42.641 --> 00:52:45.230
and more and more researcher
is also being done

00:52:45.231 --> 00:52:47.760
in terms of retention
in the tech sector,

00:52:47.761 --> 00:52:49.760
and what it actually
takes to not just

00:52:49.761 --> 00:52:52.200
ensure that we're hiring
diverse workforce,

00:52:52.201 --> 00:52:54.180
but ensure that we're
retaining them,

00:52:54.181 --> 00:52:55.360
and that they can be successful,

00:52:55.361 --> 00:52:57.200
and advance, and move up.

00:52:57.201 --> 00:52:59.340
Because obviously, as we
talked about earlier,

00:52:59.341 --> 00:53:01.070
that's good for business,

00:53:01.071 --> 00:53:03.585
and good for individual teams.

00:53:03.586 --> 00:53:05.680
But, I think inclusion has

00:53:05.681 --> 00:53:08.165
emerged as a really
important second leg.

00:53:08.166 --> 00:53:11.715
So, at first, the conversation
was all around diversity,

00:53:11.716 --> 00:53:14.120
and improving
your hiring numbers,

00:53:14.121 --> 00:53:17.210
but you also need to
retain this talent and

00:53:17.211 --> 00:53:20.355
so having an inclusive culture
is equally important.

00:53:20.356 --> 00:53:22.120
I think we see companies

00:53:22.121 --> 00:53:24.870
investing in mentorship programs,

00:53:24.871 --> 00:53:26.580
having the right policies,

00:53:26.581 --> 00:53:28.870
there are tools that are
cropping up to support

00:53:28.871 --> 00:53:31.690
that both in the hiring
process, for example,

00:53:31.691 --> 00:53:34.360
to reduce bias as well as

00:53:34.361 --> 00:53:36.920
support systems for
employees that are

00:53:36.921 --> 00:53:39.995
currently experiencing
harassment or discrimination.

00:53:39.996 --> 00:53:42.905
I mean, I think ultimately,

00:53:42.906 --> 00:53:47.300
whenever there's
more honest discussion,

00:53:47.301 --> 00:53:50.200
and people are speaking
out about these issues,

00:53:50.201 --> 00:53:54.070
and it becomes more honest,

00:53:54.071 --> 00:53:57.360
and people that have
been maybe victims

00:53:57.361 --> 00:54:00.565
of unfortunate circumstances
are able to have a voice,

00:54:00.566 --> 00:54:03.705
there's always going to
be a backlash to that.

00:54:03.706 --> 00:54:07.320
I think we need to have
the hard conversations.

00:54:07.321 --> 00:54:09.340
We need to look at the data,
and we need to be really,

00:54:09.341 --> 00:54:13.899
really proactive, and track
all of these metrics,

00:54:13.900 --> 00:54:16.940
and hold ourselves
accountable to solving

00:54:16.941 --> 00:54:18.200
these issues because it's not

00:54:18.201 --> 00:54:20.800
just a problem for
the individuals.

00:54:20.801 --> 00:54:22.290
It's a problem for the company,

00:54:22.291 --> 00:54:23.970
and it's a problem for the teams,

00:54:23.971 --> 00:54:26.060
and it's everyone's problem.

00:54:26.061 --> 00:54:28.910
I think that's
my own perspective,

00:54:28.911 --> 00:54:32.440
is that it's not just a problem
for women to solve.

00:54:32.441 --> 00:54:35.720
It's a problem that affects

00:54:35.721 --> 00:54:37.725
everyone and everyone loses

00:54:37.726 --> 00:54:39.885
if certain groups are left out.

00:54:39.886 --> 00:54:43.020
So, I hope that
the conversation continues,

00:54:43.021 --> 00:54:45.510
I hope that we're able to have

00:54:45.511 --> 00:54:48.690
the difficult and
challenging conversations

00:54:48.691 --> 00:54:50.270
that might be uncomfortable.

00:54:50.271 --> 00:54:52.990
I hope that we can address this.

00:54:52.991 --> 00:54:54.380
I think with AI, it's

00:54:54.381 --> 00:54:58.245
such an exciting moment
because, wow,

00:54:58.246 --> 00:55:02.190
we're seeing a lot of challenges,

00:55:02.191 --> 00:55:05.680
and certainly there's
a diversity crisis in the field.

00:55:05.681 --> 00:55:07.490
There's also the opportunity
to address it at

00:55:07.491 --> 00:55:09.160
these early stages and

00:55:09.161 --> 00:55:11.470
steer the train in
a better direction.

00:55:11.471 --> 00:55:13.300
So, I'm very optimistic

00:55:13.301 --> 00:55:15.185
or I wouldn't be
doing what I'm doing,

00:55:15.186 --> 00:55:16.830
but I also think we have to be

00:55:16.831 --> 00:55:19.700
proactive in all sorts of ways.

00:55:19.701 --> 00:55:22.180
It's not just going to
take one magic bullet,

00:55:22.181 --> 00:55:24.320
and it's not just going
to take one organization.

00:55:24.321 --> 00:55:26.070
It's going to take a community in

00:55:26.071 --> 00:55:28.930
our whole society
coming together and

00:55:28.931 --> 00:55:34.760
collaborating to solve
this challenge. Great question.

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:38.640
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, if that's it.

00:55:38.641 --> 00:55:40.320
Let's thank Tess.

00:55:40.321 --> 00:55:42.790
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Thank you so much.

